window.docContents[229].resolve({"/Abstraction/Properties/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Properties":{"contents":"A well typed program has a derivation, does not terminate, or is stuck. It is stuck at a case C++17 leaves undefined, at the end of a main without return, at the call of a std::function with no target, or at a failed assert. The evaluator reports it as error. C++17 defines the last two cases. The call of a std::function with no target throws bad_function_call (N4659 §23.14.13.1 ¶1), and a failed assert calls abort, . Core C++ has no exceptions, , so both end the program. Every well typed constructor has an evaluation rule, checked by inspection of the rule set. There is no Lean proof yet.\n\nEvery construct has rules with mutually exclusive premises, and a Core C++ program has at most one result. The evaluation order fixed left to right removes the choice C++17 leaves to the compiler. The relations of  make the statement precise. Two derivations of  for the same  give the same  and . The same holds for LEval, Exec, Execs and the auxiliary dynamic relations. The proof in CoreCpp/Semantics/Determinism.lean is one mutual structural recursion on the first derivation. Each case either applies the rule of the second derivation or contradicts one of its premises.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Abstraction","header":"5.6. Properties","id":"/Abstraction/Properties/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Properties"}});